---
import DeepDiveLayout from "../components/redesign/DeepDiveLayout.astro";
import pii from "../../public/data/pii_latest.json";

function pct(n, d = 2) { return (n === null || n === undefined || Number.isNaN(n)) ? "n/a" : (n * 100).toFixed(d) + "%"; }
const WALLET_LABEL: Record<string, string> = {
  bot: "Algorithmic",
  active_retail: "Active Retail",
  sophisticated: "Sophisticated",
  casual: "Casual",
  one_shot: "One-Shot",
};
function walletLabel(k: string) { return WALLET_LABEL[k] ?? k; }

const ed = pii.event_detection;
const sk = pii.sustained_skill;
const f = ed.funnel;
const sv = ed.survivors;

const funnelRows = [
  { label: "Raw flags (p < 0.01, independence null)", n: f.raw_flags_p_lt_01 },
  { label: "Excluded: mutually exclusive (neg-risk) events", n: f.excluded_mutually_exclusive },
  { label: "Defensible (independence admissible)", n: f.defensible },
  { label: "Distinct-question core", n: f.distinct_question_core },
  { label: `Survive dependence adjustment (5% level, ρ = ${sv.icc_rho})`, n: sv.at_5pct_dependence_adjusted },
];
const fMax = f.raw_flags_p_lt_01;

const lede = `Across ${ed.sample.testable_pairs.toLocaleString()} testable trader-event pairs (${ed.sample.traders.toLocaleString()} traders, ${ed.sample.events.toLocaleString()} multi-market events), the per-event joint-accuracy test produces ${f.raw_flags_p_lt_01.toLocaleString()} raw flags. Excluding mutually exclusive events and dependent bet structures leaves a distinct-question core of ${f.distinct_question_core} trader-event pairs, of which ${sv.at_5pct_dependence_adjusted} survive the within-event dependence adjustment at the five percent level and ${sv.at_1pct_dependence_adjusted} at one percent. On a placebo class where episodic private information is implausible, the corrected test yields zero discoveries.`;
---
<DeepDiveLayout
  title="Private Information Index (PII)"
  description="Per-event detection of trading patterns consistent with episodic private information on Polymarket, aligned with Della Vedova (2026), 'One Event at a Time'."
  eyebrow="DV-PMI · Insider screen"
  lede={lede}
  citeKey="dellavedova2026pii"
  citeTitle="Private Information Index"
  citePath="pii">

  <Fragment slot="chart">
    <div role="img" aria-label="Detection funnel from raw flags to dependence-adjusted survivors">
      {funnelRows.map(r => (
        <div style="margin:6px 0;">
          <div style="font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:13px;color:var(--muted);">{r.label}</div>
          <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:10px;">
            <div style={`height:16px;background:var(--accent);opacity:0.85;width:${Math.max(0.6, (r.n / fMax) * 100)}%;`}></div>
            <span class="mono" style="font-size:13px;">{r.n.toLocaleString()}</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="caption">
    The detection funnel. Each stage removes a source of false positives: platform-defined mutually exclusive
    (neg-risk) events, dependent bet structures (single-game multi-bets, cumulative threshold ladders), and
    residual within-event correlation (beta-binomial adjustment at intraclass correlation {sv.icc_rho},
    estimated from the data).
  </Fragment>

  <h2>What this index measures</h2>
  <p>
    Detecting informed trading requires separating transient information from persistent skill, and the
    separation cannot be made at the trader level: pooled averages dilute episodic information, and the
    best-episode statistic inflates mechanically with activity. The test therefore operates at the
    trader-event unit. For each trader and each multi-market event where the trader took positions in at
    least two component markets, the joint probability of the observed directional accuracy is evaluated
    against the price-implied null: a trader with no information does no better than the price she paid.
    Multiplicity is controlled across the events each trader contests (Holm within trader).
  </p>

  <h2>Survivors by disclosure archetype</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Archetype</th><th>Pairs</th></tr>
    {sv.by_archetype.map(a => (
      <tr>
        <td>{a.archetype}</td>
        <td class="mono">{a.n}</td>
      </tr>
    ))}
    <tr><th>Total (5% level)</th><th class="mono">{sv.at_5pct_dependence_adjusted}</th></tr>
  </table>
  <p>
    All survivors are all-correct records on disclosure events (adjusted p-values {sv.adjusted_p_min} to
    {sv.adjusted_p_max}). They are reported by archetype only; the dashboard names no wallets. Flags mark
    statistical patterns consistent with informed trading, not legal determinations. Net directional profit
    across the {ed.core_profits.pairs_covered} core pairs with position coverage is
    ${ed.core_profits.total_usd.toLocaleString()} (median ${ed.core_profits.median_usd_per_pair} per pair).
  </p>

  <h2>Placebo calibration</h2>
  <p>
    The false-positive rate is calibrated on a real-data placebo: {ed.placebo.pairs.toLocaleString()} testable
    pairs in asset-price-direction markets, where episodic private information about the fundamental is
    implausible. The corrected test yields {ed.placebo.corrected_discoveries} discoveries on the placebo at
    every threshold examined. An uncorrected best-episode rule, by contrast, flags active traders at a rate
    that rises mechanically with how many events they trade.
  </p>

  <h2>Forward test</h2>
  <p>
    {ed.forward_test} The wallet mapping is the author's inference from public on-chain data, not a fact
    stated in the complaint.
  </p>

  <h2>Sustained-skill monitor (reclassified wallet-level screen)</h2>
  <p>
    {sk.reclassification_note}
  </p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Wallet class</th><th>Wallets tested</th><th>Flagged (p &lt; 0.01)</th><th>Flag rate</th></tr>
    {sk.by_wallet_type.map(t => (
      <tr>
        <td>{walletLabel(t.wallet_type)}</td>
        <td class="mono">{t.n_wallets.toLocaleString()}</td>
        <td class="mono">{t.n_significant_01.toLocaleString()}</td>
        <td class="mono">{pct(t.flag_rate, 2)}</td>
      </tr>
    ))}
    <tr><th>Total</th><th class="mono">{sk.total_wallets_tested.toLocaleString()}</th><th class="mono">{sk.total_flagged_p_lt_01.toLocaleString()}</th><th class="mono">{pct(sk.flag_rate, 2)}</th></tr>
  </table>
  <p>
    Of the {sk.total_flagged_p_lt_01.toLocaleString()} wallets with sustained excess accuracy at the
    one-percent threshold, {sk.holm_bonferroni_survivors.toLocaleString()} survive the Holm-Bonferroni
    family-wise correction and {sk.bh_fdr_survivors.toLocaleString()} survive the Benjamini-Hochberg FDR
    procedure. {sk.data_note}
  </p>

  <h2>Methodology</h2>
  <p>
    {ed.method} Event groupings and the mutually-exclusive attribute come from the platform's own market
    metadata; disclosure archetypes are assigned by a rules-and-language-model classification validated
    against hand-coded markets. Full construction, proofs, and robustness are in the source paper.
  </p>

  <h2>Source paper</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=6567238">
      Della Vedova, J. (2026). Detecting Informed Trading in Prediction Markets: One Event at a Time. SSRN 6567238, revised June 2026.
    </a>
    The companion paper, <a href="/research">Picking Winners and Losing Money: The Two Dimensions of Trading
    Skill</a>, establishes the accuracy-execution orthogonality that this paper inverts into an
    identification problem.
  </p>

  <h2>Related surveillance indices</h2>
  <p>
    PII is the first of eight DV-PMI surveillance indices. The companion indices test for pre-resolution
    timing concentration, spread patterns in markets with flagged wallets, and the response of flagged-wallet
    excess accuracy to resolution surprises. <a href="/surveillance">See the full surveillance suite &rarr;</a>
  </p>

  <h2>Data</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/data/pii_snapshot.csv">pii_snapshot.csv</a>
    &middot;
    <a href="/data/pii_latest.json">pii_latest.json</a>
  </p>
</DeepDiveLayout>
